<p>There is a version of automation that makes people cringe. You know the one. You fill out a form, and within 0.3 seconds you receive an email that says "Hi &#123;FirstName&#125;, thanks for your interest!" followed by a wall of text that clearly has nothing to do with you as a person. Then another one the next day. And another. Until you unsubscribe or silently add that business to your mental blacklist.</p>

<p>That is not automation. That is spam wearing a polo shirt.</p>

<p>Real automation, the kind that actually converts leads into clients, feels like a thoughtful human remembered to follow up at exactly the right time. It feels personal because, done right, it is personal. The system handles the timing and the delivery. You handle the message and the intent.</p>

<p>Here is how to build follow-up sequences that people actually appreciate.</p>

<div class="divider">...</div>

<h2>Start with timing, not content</h2>

<p>The biggest mistake people make with automated follow-up is obsessing over what to say before thinking about when to say it. Timing matters more than wording. A perfectly written email that arrives at the wrong moment gets ignored. A simple, conversational message that arrives at the right moment gets a reply.</p>

<p>Here is a timing framework that works for most service-based businesses:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Immediately after enquiry:</strong> A short confirmation. Not a sales pitch. Just acknowledgment that you received their message and a rough timeline for when they will hear from you.</li>
  <li><strong>2-4 hours later:</strong> A personal follow-up. This is where you add value. Answer their likely question. Share a relevant resource. Show that you understand what they are looking for.</li>
  <li><strong>48 hours later:</strong> A gentle check-in. Did they have any questions? Is there anything you can help clarify? Keep it to two or three sentences.</li>
  <li><strong>5-7 days later:</strong> A soft nudge with social proof. Mention a recent result or a quick case study. Not pushy. Just relevant.</li>
  <li><strong>14 days later:</strong> A graceful close. Let them know you are still here if they need anything, and leave the door open.</li>
</ul>

<p>Five touchpoints over two weeks. That is it. Most businesses either follow up once and give up, or follow up ten times in three days and burn the bridge. The sweet spot is in between.</p>

<h2>Write like you are texting a friend (who asked for your help)</h2>

<p>The fastest way to make automated messages feel robotic is to write them like marketing copy. Drop the corporate language. Drop the exclamation marks. Drop the "we are delighted to inform you" phrasing that nobody uses in real conversation.</p>

<p>Instead, write the way you would actually speak to someone. Here is the difference:</p>

<p><strong>Robotic:</strong> "Thank you for your enquiry! We are thrilled to connect with you. Our team of experts is ready to help you achieve your business goals. Please find attached our comprehensive service brochure."</p>

<p><strong>Human:</strong> "Hey Sarah, thanks for reaching out. I had a quick look at your website and I think there are a few easy wins we could tackle. Happy to jump on a call this week if you are free. No pressure at all."</p>

<p>The second version takes 10 seconds to read and actually sounds like it came from a person. That is what you are aiming for.</p>

<blockquote><p>Automation should handle the when. You should handle the how it sounds.</p></blockquote>

<h2>Personalisation beyond first names</h2>

<p>Using someone's first name in an email is table stakes. Every tool does it. Every spam email does it too. Real personalisation goes deeper.</p>

<p>Most platforms, especially a good <a href="/blog/crm-small-business">CRM</a>, let you use dynamic fields based on what you know about the contact. Here are some that actually make a difference:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>The service they enquired about.</strong> "You mentioned you were looking at our website design packages" hits differently than "Thanks for your interest in our services."</li>
  <li><strong>Their location.</strong> "Since you are based in the Inner West, you might like to know we have worked with a few businesses around Marrickville." That is hyper-relevant.</li>
  <li><strong>How they found you.</strong> "I saw you came through from our Google listing" tells them you are paying attention.</li>
  <li><strong>The day of the week.</strong> A Monday morning message reads differently than a Friday afternoon one. Use conditional logic to adjust tone.</li>
</ul>

<p>The goal is not to be creepy. The goal is to make every message feel like it was written for that person, even though the system wrote it once and sends it to everyone who matches the criteria.</p>

<h2>SMS vs email: when to use which</h2>

<p>This is where a lot of businesses get it wrong. They default to <a href="/blog/email-marketing-small-business">email</a> for everything because it feels safer. But <a href="/blog/sms-marketing-small-business">SMS has a 98% open rate</a> compared to email's 20-30%. For certain messages, SMS is not just better. It is the only channel that works.</p>

<p>Here is a simple rule of thumb:</p>

<p><strong>Use SMS for:</strong></p>
<ul>
  <li>Appointment confirmations and reminders</li>
  <li>Short check-ins after a service</li>
  <li>Review requests (the link needs to be one tap away)</li>
  <li>Time-sensitive follow-ups where you need a quick response</li>
</ul>

<p><strong>Use email for:</strong></p>
<ul>
  <li>Longer nurture content (tips, guides, case studies)</li>
  <li>Proposals and quotes</li>
  <li>Newsletters and updates</li>
  <li>Anything that benefits from formatting, images, or links</li>
</ul>

<p>The best sequences use both. An email with detail, followed by an SMS a day later that says "Hey, just checking you saw my email. Let me know if you have questions." That one-two punch converts better than either channel alone.</p>

