<h2>The "if/then" framework</h2>

<p>Not every lead should get the same sequence. Someone who opened every email and clicked your pricing page is in a very different headspace than someone who has not opened a single message. Your automation should reflect that.</p>

<p>Build branches into your sequences:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>If they opened your email but did not reply:</strong> Send a shorter, more direct follow-up. They are interested but not convinced.</li>
  <li><strong>If they clicked a link to your pricing page:</strong> Skip the nurture content and offer to book a call. They are ready to talk numbers.</li>
  <li><strong>If they have not opened anything:</strong> Try a different subject line or switch to SMS. The message might be fine. The delivery channel might be wrong.</li>
  <li><strong>If they replied:</strong> Stop the automation immediately. Nothing kills trust faster than receiving an automated message after you have already started a real conversation.</li>
</ul>

<p>This is where automation gets genuinely powerful. You are not just sending messages on a timer. You are responding to behaviour. And that is what makes it feel human.</p>

<blockquote><p>The best automation is invisible. Your lead should never feel like they are in a sequence. They should feel like they are in a conversation.</p></blockquote>

<h2>One last thing: know when to stop</h2>

<p>Every sequence needs an endpoint. If someone has not engaged after five or six touchpoints over two to three weeks, they are not ready. A solid <a href="/blog/customer-follow-up-system">customer follow-up system</a> knows when to push and when to pause. And that is fine. Add them to a long-term nurture list. Send them something valuable once a month. But stop the active follow-up.</p>

<p>Respecting someone's silence is one of the most trust-building things a business can do. When they are ready, they will come back. And when they do, your system will be there to pick up exactly where it left off.</p>

